      "summary": "Deliberation is often understood as the process of examining one’s thoughts to reach a clearer understanding of reality. However, this raises a question of how individuals rec",
      "html": "<div style=\"white-space:pre-line\">Deliberation is often understood as the process of examining one’s thoughts to reach a\r\nclearer understanding of reality. However, this raises a question of how individuals recognize\r\nthat their current understanding is incomplete or mistaken. In Meditations on First Philosophy,\r\nRené Descartes approaches deliberation through systematic doubt, questioning everything that\r\ncannot be known with certainty. Similarly, in The Republic, Plato uses the Allegory of the Cave\r\nto illustrate how individuals must move beyond their limited perceptions to recognize a greater\r\nreality. Although they approach this process differently, both suggest that deliberation requires\r\nmore than questioning existing beliefs. Instead, it relies on a standard beyond one’s current\r\nunderstanding to recognize limitations and move towards something better.\r\nDescartes presents deliberation as the elimination of doubtful beliefs. He leaves aside\r\nanything open to doubt and imagines that a powerful deceiver misleads him about everything\r\n(17). Through this process, he concludes that his existence must be certain whenever he thinks,\r\nsince even a deceived mind must exist to be deceived. The wax passage extends this\r\nreasoning. Although the wax changes in shape, smell, and texture, Descartes judges that it\r\nremains the same wax. He concludes that understanding the wax depends on the mind’s\r\njudgment rather than sensory perception alone (20–21). Deliberation therefore removes\r\nwhatever cannot withstand doubt until the thinking subject remains as the first certainty. This\r\nprocess is difficult, as Descartes describes himself as tumbling in a whirlpool in which he can\r\nneither stand nor swim (16). Nevertheless, he initiates the process himself.\r\nIn contrast, Plato presents deliberation as something initiated from outside the individual.\r\nHis prisoners are able to discuss what they see and even compete to predict which shadow will\r\nappear next, but their reasoning is limited by appearances because they mistake the shadows\r\nfor reality itself (515b–516d). The freed prisoner does not choose his release; someone drags\r\nhim up the steep ascent by force. Even then, he initially believes that the shadows were more\r\nreal than the objects he now sees (515d–e). Whereas Descartes reasons his way toward\r\ncertainty through an individual act of questioning, Plato suggests that understanding requires\r\nthe prisoner to be turned around, guided, and gradually accustomed to a new reality. This\r\ndistinction sharpens the contrast between the two authors: for Descartes, deliberation is a\r\nmethod one undertakes, while for Plato, it is a transformation one undergoes.\r\nDespite these differences, both accounts suggest that deliberation depends on a\r\nmeasure the mind does not produce independently. This is consistent with Descartes carrying\r\nout his doubt alone in the first two Mediations. The question is not who does the deliberating but\r\nwhat makes the process recognizable as deliberation. Descartes’ doubt shows a sense of\r\nsomething missing: he recognizes his uncertainty as a limitation, which he only understands in\r\nrelation to the idea of something finished. He makes this connection clear in the Third\r\nMediation, when he asks how he could recognize his own doubt and imperfection without\r\npossessing an idea of a more perfect being (31). The cogito shows that he exists as a doubter,\r\nbut this passage shows that being a doubter is already a way of falling short of something.\r\nPlato’s freed prisoner experiences a similar process. The shadows only become recognizable\r\nas shadows once he encounters the sun, which Plato presents as the source of everything he\r\nnow sees (516b-c). This allowed me to understand that a standard does more than simply judgewhat is right or wrong. It also provides an option for change. The prisoner can only turn away\r\nfrom the shadows because he has something else to turn toward. Descartes’ idea of perfection\r\nserves a similar purpose. It gives his doubt a direction by providing a standard against which he\r\ncan recognize his own limitations. However, while Plato places this standard outside the\r\nprisoner, something he must be guided toward, Descartes finds it within the mind while arguing\r\nthat he could not create it himself. In both situations, deliberation involves moving toward\r\nsomething the individual has not yet attained.\r\nThis connection challenges my previous understanding of deliberation as simply\r\nconsciousness examining its own thoughts. Self-examination alone cannot explain how\r\nquestioning our beliefs leads to better understanding, Descartes removes beliefs, while Plato’s\r\nprisoner moves beyond illusions, but both rely on what is better or more complete. This shows\r\nthat deliberation is not just about removing wrong beliefs or changing one's perspective; it\r\nrequires a standard for recognizing progress. Although Descartes and Palto disagree about\r\nwhere this standard comes from, both suggest that questioning our beliefs needs something to\r\nguide us. Deliberation, therefore, is the mind comparing its understanding to something beyond\r\nitself.</div>",
